Nge7 {The king's knight, at his king's second square.} 14. O-O {The king castles with his rook.}
Nf5 {The king's knight, at his bishop's fourth square. (b) NOTE: He plays this knight to get rid
of your bishop, and open afterwards a free passage for his king's bishop upon your king and
queen; which would prove very dangerous. -} 15. Rad1 {The queen's rook, at its queen's square.
(c) NOTE: You play this rook, with a view either to oblige him to sustain his queen's pawn, or
to take your queen's knight.} Nxe3 {The king's knight takes the bishop.} 16. Qxe3 {The queen
retakes the knight.} Ne7 {The queen's knight, at his king's second square.} 17. Kh1 {The king,
at his rook's square. (d) NOTE: You might have taken his queen's pawn with your knight, and
afterwards played your queen to your king's fourth square; but, to force his knight, he would
then have disentangled his queen, by giving check: so that this move is absolutely necessary.}
Bxc3 {The king's bishop takes the knight. (e) NOTE: As he can no more sustain his queen's pawn,
he finds himself under the necessity of taking your knight, to preserve that pawn.} 18. bxc3
{The queen's knight's pawn retakes the bishop.} O-O {The king castles. (f) NOTE: In this
situation, the white must win the game: the matter is now, only to hinder the adverse queen's
pawn from entering again upon action, and to sustain your centre without being too hasty in
advancing your pawns.} *
